{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lpmb8F8uhRG8ec6Vg1DKrM","type":"tool_result","content":"b5761ee6 D28 technique logs: binary re-run (50 turns), metadata delivery-layouts section restored\n6fa70fff D27 post-run output: prebuild breadth, day27 solvers, coverage + technique logs\ne80f0e69 D26 post-run output: lib ratchet, prebuild modules + fixtures (46/50 kept), technique logs, coverage atlas, day26 solvers, D26 web plugins\n# D30 \"SHA1-Hulud\" — what we missed, and what to build\n\nDay 30 bonus binary (`binary-reverse-engineering-day-30-sha1-hulud`): a hardened Rust PE64 crackme. One answer\n(`flag`), 3 wrong GM submissions, per-team build (own flag). Our workers: 4 launches, ~1 h 45 min, no flag. A reviewer's\nmanual analysis (`challenges/day30/binary-sha1-hulud/single_solve/manual/`) solves it in ~5 min of wall clock. The\norganizers say this class is Day-30 only, so the value is the **method**, not the solver.\n\n## How the challenge works (reviewer manual analysis)\n\n1. **Self-integrity.** SHA-1 of its own `.text` (+68 B of PE metadata) seeds the keys; a ~11 s pseudo-random walk over\n   the same bytes folds in too. Patching any `.text` byte silently poisons every key.\n2. **Anti-debug.** ThreadHideFromDebugger, PEB/NtGlobalFlag, debug port/object/flags, kernel debugger, DR registers.\n3. **Environment fingerprint feeds the KEYS.** CPUID hypervisor bit, CPU count < 4, memory, display size, registry and\n   driver markers, 37 analysis-tool process names. On an analysis box every input, **including the right flag**, prints\n   nothing. The binary is not an oracle there.\n4. **Check.** `<u64 len><lowercased input>` → 16-round block cipher (CBC, per-block key derived from plaintext block j-1)\n   → second 16-round pass running **backwards** → constant-time XOR fold against a stored target. The verdict ORs about\n   18 groups, so **several strings print `correct`**: decoys (\"your flag is in another castle\", a short `flag{thi…`).\n5. **Attack.** `key_0` and the layer-2 key do not depend on the input, so any probe decodes block 0 (length + 8 chars).\n   Reconstruct the target as `buffer ^ bl ^ sil ^ dl` at the compare (0x59cd9), then make 3 progressive probes\n   (8 → 24 → 38 chars). Pick the `flag{<32 hex>}` group.\n\n## What our workers did (timeline)\n\n| launch | turns / time | path | outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| answer-first x3 | ~12-20 calls each | static XOR-pair strings, `.xsobf` decoder | stopped correctly, 0 submits (good) |\n| single 1 | 81 / 25 min | Unicorn emulator of main; **stubbed env/API resolver to \"not found = clean\"** | \"emulation runs to completion but prints no verdict\" → read as a gate bailing out |\n| single 2 | 75 / 25 min | modelled the 4 self-decrypting SHA-1 stubs (2 of them tweaked) | rabbit hole: the hash only needs to run correctly, not to be modelled |\n| single 3 | 81 turns (cap) | \"input never appears in a SHA-1 block → transformed first\" | right observation, wrong frame (looked in the hash, not a cipher) |\n| single 4 | running | found the 1,200-byte compare vs a constant table; mapping input → compared bytes | byte-at-a-time attack, which the backwards layer-2 diffusion defeats |\n\nCoordinator hint 1 was right: hook the compare at 0x140059cd9 and diff operands across single-byte-varied inputs.\nHint 2 pointed at the stubs. Neither said that **silence means poisoned keys**.\n\n## What we missed (method)\n\n1. **No-oracle blindness.** We took \"no output\" as \"the gate failed\". Rule: when an emulated or sandboxed crackme is\n   silent on every input, assume the environment checks are feeding the keys **before** assuming your input is wrong.\n   Find the fingerprint routines (CPUID 0x40000000, GetSystemInfo / GlobalMemoryStatusEx / GetSystemMetrics, registry,\n   process lists, tool-name tables) and make them report a **plausible real host**: 4+ CPUs, 8 GB, 1920x1080, no\n   hypervisor bit, no tool processes. A \"clean / not found\" stub is not neutral. It is a value, and it goes into the keys.\n2. **Differential instrumentation before reversing.** Their key move is measurement: run two different candidates and\n   diff every stream at the compare. Streams that stay identical across inputs are the stored target and masks. Then\n   ask which keys depend on the input (`key_0` and layer-2 did not). We reversed top-down; they measured.\n3. **Chosen-plaintext progressive decode.** When block j's key depends on plaintext block j-1, decode block 0 from any\n   probe, then extend the probe with what you learned. Probe length must equal the target's length word.\n4. **Decoys under an OR verdict.** \"Prints correct\" is necessary, not sufficient. Enumerate every accepted group and\n   submit only the flag-shaped one. Never spend one of the 3 wrong submissions on the first hit.\n5. **Self-hash discipline.** Never byte-patch `.text`. Use emulator code hooks, or shadow `.text` and repoint the reader.\n   Writable `.data` (lazy-import cache slots) is the safe place to hook.\n6. **Don't model what you only need to execute.** Launch 2 spent 25 min fitting SHA-1 variants. Correct execution was\n   enough; speed comes from skipping the deliberate slow loop, not from re-implementing the hash.\n\n## What we missed (harness)\n\n1. **Work lost across relaunches.** A worker's `$TMPDIR` (`.run/tmp/single-*`) sits outside the challenge dir. I\n   copied launch 1 and 2 by hand into `prior*/`. Fix: point single workers' scratch at `<chal>/single_solve/work/scratch`,\n   or rsync it into the chal dir on exit or kill.\n2. **Turn and time caps end a converging worker.** 80 turns / 25 min stopped launches that were still progressing. Fix:\n   on `max_turns` or timeout with fresh notes.md progress, auto-relaunch (resume) with notes + scratch, up to N times,\n   instead of ending the chain.\n3. **Wrong terminal state.** After launch 1 timed out the chain went `stage=done`, and `hctf why` printed\n   \"DONE - all modes solved\" with all 3 modes pending. The post-run gate caught it (HELD), but the dashboard lied.\n4. **Coordinator playbook.** Hints should carry the method checklist above (silence = poisoned keys; differential runs;\n   decoys), not just the next address.\n5. **Ghidra path bug.** headless failed on a `.run` path component; the worker lost a few minutes re-running it.\n\n## Tools\n\n- **Speakeasy** (Mandiant): Windows PE emulator with built-in API, PEB and TEB stubs and no Windows DLLs. It would have\n  saved launch 1 most of its scaffolding. Candidate for `setup/install_tools.sh` (standard tool, per the LLM-first rule).\n- Unicorn hooks remain the right capture vehicle on Linux: no native execution of challenge binaries on the host.\n\nDay 31 has only the usual lanes, so the harness items (1-3) matter more generally than the crackme method does.\n{\n  \"base\": \"binary-reverse-engineering-day-30-sha1-hulud\",\n  \"lane\": \"binary-reverse-engineering\",\n  \"name\": \"⚙️ SHA1-Hulud\",\n  \"ids\": {\n    \"single_solve\": \"binary-reverse-engineering-day-30-sha1-hulud\"\n  }\n}{\n  \"stage\": \"done\",\n  \"name\": \"\\u2699\\ufe0f SHA1-Hulud\",\n  \"lane\": \"binary-reverse-engineering\",\n  \"worker\": null,\n  \"updated\": \"2026-10-31T11:38:53\",\n  \"launches\": 1,\n  \"help\": \"# HELP \\u2014 binary-sha1-hulud single_solve (answer-first worker, attempt 9eb0070b)\\n\\nNo flag submitted (0/3 wrong-flag budget used). Blocked: the flag check runs in a custom bytecode VM; deriving it needs\\na VM lift / emulation job, beyond an answer-first budget.\\n\\n## Facts from the bytes (sha1-hulud.exe, Rust PE32+)\\n- embre `crypt::xor` = fcn.14005dc8a ({src,len,key,len}); .rdata pairs give \\\"What is the flag?\\\" (0x1400883c0^0x1400883d1, 17)\\n  and \\\"correct, the flag is \\\" (0x1400883e6^0x1400883fb, 21).\\n- Rust main = 0x14003c38a. stdin line -> trim \\\\n\\\\r -> rbp+0x1148/0x4d0; success branch 0x14005a02b prints prefix + input,\\n  so the flag is the accepted input (not printed from a constant).\\n- `.xsobf` (0x14009e000) = obfuscated strings; decoder fcn.14006397c (struct {ptr,len,tag}); reimplemented in\\n  `xsobf_dec.py` (verified: 0x14009e008/0x21/tag 0xc250001c -> \\\"SYSTEM\\\\CurrentControlSet\\\\Services\\\", section+0/8/0xc260001c -> \\\"SOFTWARE\\\").\\n  These are anti-sandbox strings (registry, process/driver names), not the flag.\\n- Big anti-analysis gauntlet: PEB BeingDebugged, hashed API resolver fcn.140064480 (0x9394fb88 module hash), CPUID 0x40000000,\\n  RDTSC, screen-size, registry enumeration with FNV-like/splitmix hash compares vs tables 0x140088320/0x140088200/0x140088350.\\n- Inline SHA-1 at 0x14003de9a over a section found by fcn.14003bca2 (self-integrity).\\n- Input check @0x14004a5bc: input is trimmed + ASCII-lowercased, then fed to a VM: bytecode 0x4000 B at 0x14007fa50\\n  (LEB128 operands, `& 0x\"\n}# sha1-hulud single_solve: answer-first worker stopped (call budget), 0 flag submissions made\n\nStatic findings (sha1-hulud.exe, Rust PE64):\n- `.xsobf` strings: struct {ptr,len,seed}, decoder at 0x14006397c: s=rol(seed,17)^0x5d2e9c47a1b386f0, counter c+=0x065767ac2ba816c3,\n  z=c^s; z=(z^z>>29)*0x41169ddc6c156db1; z=(z^z>>27)*0xa3d1f5bb9847a411; byte=((z>>55)^(z>>24))&0xff ^ data[i].\n  Decodes to anti-analysis strings only (registry Services/SOFTWARE, \\drivers, x64dbg/ida/procmon/... process list).\n- embre xor (0x14005dc8a) pairs: 0x1400883c0^0x1400883d1 (17) = \"What is the flag?\"; 0x1400883e6^0x1400883fb (21) = \"correct, the flag is \".\n- Success prints \"correct, the flag is \" + the user INPUT (rbp+0x180): the flag is the input accepted by the checker.\n- Checker is in main 0x14003c38a..0x14005a1fa (~27k insns): trims \\n\\r, lowercases A-Z (0x14004a6c8), uses a 0x4000-byte blob at\n  0x14007fa50 with varint-decoded ops (0x14004aab1), multiple SHA-1 IV inits (0x67452301 at 0x14003cbbd, 0x14003d546, ...),\n  16-slot loop (rbp+0x7c8 to 0x10). Anti-debug results (PEB BeingDebugged, NtQueryInformationProcess classes 7/0x1e/0x1f, process list) are\n  folded into state, so an emulator must spoof a clean environment.\nNext step: reverse the VM/blob check (or emulate with clean anti-debug answers) to recover the accepted input.\n- sha1-hulud.exe: Rust PE64, obfuscated main 0x14003c38a (API-hash resolver 0x140064480, .xsobf strings, anti-debug/VM, SMC via VirtualProtect, bytecode VM 0x4000 @0x14007fa50) | prints \"correct, the flag is <input>\" | emulating main in unicorn ($TMPDIR/emu/emu.py)\n- emu: slow loop 0x14004194d replaced by C ($TMPDIR/emu/slow); emu reaches NtReadFile then 8k+ SHA1-compress SMC calls, at 0x14001ba63 after 60s\n- D30 resume: 0x14001dfc0 stub = straight-line interp of deduped native trace (emu/trace6d.pkl, TRLEN=d87f) -> 4/4 pairs match; 0x14002b840 model (brot=2,chx20) verified natively. emu/emu7.py = full emu with all 4 stubs fast.\n- L4: emu11.py (prior2/emu, slowbuf race FIXED: per-pid) dumps cipher state. Check = len8||lower(input) in 16B blocks -> pass1 CBC 128-bit ARX cipher (K1_k from SHA1 chain over prev plaintext blocks) -> pass2 reverse-order chained cipher (K2, r12) -> ^KS -> compare vs E=sil^dl. Python model+inverse in $TMPDIR/s30/ciph.py, inv.py (verified: inverting V gives the input).\n- L4: inverting E for all 21 groups (3 epochs x 7 groups n=3,6,3,3,2,2,6) -> GARBAGE. Epochs = duplicate pairs among 16 slots qwords rbp+0x38..0xb8 (outer loop 0x14004a833, match 0x14004a8b0) -> env-check tokens? hypothesis: emulator env answers poison slots/keys.\n- L4: ENV FEEDS KEYS (not just bails): coherent clocks (emu18: VT virtual time, rdtsc=300x QPC, KUSER updated) changed timing verdict 0x14003adfb -> 0 and changed all keys/E. emu20 adds RtlDecompressBuffer(LZNT1, hash 0x97e5cf42, ntdll 0x9394fb88; 2 calls, 0x42d->0x1d43), VirtualAlloc, drivers FindFirstFileW list, toolhelp process list, SMBIOS/ACPI fakes. Still garbage -> some env answer still unrealistic. IsDebuggerPresent=1 -> bail; cpuid HV bit -> no effect.\n- Success = [rbp+0x5f8] (any group V^KS==E) at 0x14005a012; final anti-analysis bools (0x14003c33b) were clean in emu.\n- L4 BREAKTHROUGH: emu38.py (prior2/emu) = clean env: pair-oracle hooks (obs vs exp at the 0x6161c4ba7c2419b8 mixing loops) all 104 match; fixes: TEB+0x68 LastError, VirtualAlloc(0,0)->87, VirtualFree(0)->487, page-protect+AV->VEH, KUSER XState 0x3d8=3 (=xgetbv), PEB/KUSER versions+cpu counts, fake ntdll.dll on disk (CreateFileW+NtReadFile) with 4c8bd1b8+SSN stubs, direct-syscall hook (NtQueryInformationProcess cls0 / NtQueryVirtualMemory), env-detector frame clearing at CloseHandle returns 0x14003f3ad/0x140045b46 (manual analysis), busy-loop rdtsc sites 0x14003f72f/0x140045f06 adv(40) so delta>=0x200.\n- inv.py E on d28.json: group 7 len 38 'flag{a9f...' (real), group 10 'your fla' decoy, group 11 len14 'flag{nic' decoy. Next: probe passes 2/3 (prefix-carrying probe of len 38).\n# Triage: binary-reverse-engineering-day-30-sha1-hulud\n\n**Prompt:** I made this challenge because I was really upset that TeamPCP dropped the ball with this payload name. Anyway, here's Wonder Wall!\n\n> The password for this archive is **`infected`**.\n\n**GM guidance (event-wide — note the sanctioned transport and the untrusted-data rule):**\n> Your AI Homebase (/home/HuntressCTF) is your workshop. Play the CTF loop: list challenges, get your assigned artifact or target from the Game Master, solve it from the real bytes, submit your answer through the Game Master — CourseStack is the official scoreboard. Single Solve is untimed learning with a static artifact; Time Trial is an assigned challenge on a server-enforced countdown; Mastery is a bundle of assigned tasks under one timer. Day 01–31 across six lanes — Warmups, Metadata Forensics, Script Analysis, Mobile Android, Binary, Web Exploitation (Welcome Robots is Day 1 orientation). Web Exploitation has no downloadable artifact: each started attempt returns its live target sites in targets[] (open each url); each Web challenge has its own live